General elections were held in [[Chile]] on 16 November 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 October 2025 |title=Preparations for elections in Chile move forward |url=https://www.plenglish.com/news/2025/10/24/preparations-for-elections-in-chile-move-forward/ |access-date=28 October 2025 |website=Prensa Latina |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=¿Qué se vota en las Elecciones 2025? - Gob.cl |url=https://www.gob.cl/noticias/que-se-vota-elecciones-2025/ |access-date=28 October 2025 |website=Gobierno de Chile |language=es}}</ref> Voters went to the polls to elect the [[List of presidents of Chile|38th]] [[president of Chile]], renew all 155 seats in the [[Chamber of Deputies of Chile|Chamber of Deputies]], and fill 23 of the 50 seats in the [[Senate of Chile|Senate]]. [[Republican Party of Chile|Republican Party]] candidate [[José Antonio Kast]] defeated [[Communist Party of Chile|Communist Party]] member [[Jeannette Jara]] in a runoff election on 14 December.

In the first round, Jara, a member of the [[Communist Party of Chile]],<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Malinowski |first1=Matthew |last2=Garip |first2=Patricia |date=2025-11-05 |title=Lifelong Communist Goes Mainstream in Bid for Chile's Top Job |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-05/the-communist-aiming-for-chile-s-presidency-wants-a-rebrand |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251105192347/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-11-05/the-communist-aiming-for-chile-s-presidency-wants-a-rebrand |archive-date=2025-11-05 |website=Bloomberg |quote=Chile's leading presidential candidate Jeannette Jara is a communist. Not that kind of communist, she wants you to know. [...] "Maybe that doesn't sound communist, and I don't dress that way either. But I want everyone in Chile to have the same opportunity. And that's what it means to be a communist in Chile", Jara said.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=McGowan |first1=Charis |last2=Boddenberg |first2=Sophia |date=2025-11-14 |title=Who is Jeannette Jara, the communist leading Chile's presidential election? |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/longform/2025/11/14/who-is-jeannette-jara-the-communist-leading-chiles-presidential-election |access-date=2025-12-17 |website=Al Jazeera |language=en |quote=Her presidential bid is historic. But can Jara, a communist nominated to represent the governing coalition, overcome the conservative opposition?}}</ref><ref name="LAR-20251117">{{Cite web |last=Brettkelly |first=Louis |date=17 November 2025 |title=Far-right and communist candidates reach presidential runoff in Chile |url=https://latinamericareports.com/far-right-and-communist-candidates-to-make-for-polarized-run-off-election-in-chile/12907/ |access-date=17 December 2025 |website=Latin America Reports |language=en |quote=A communist government minister and a far-right former lawmaker have progressed into a polarizing presidential runoff in Chile after winning the most votes in first round elections on Sunday.}}</ref> won a [[plurality (voting)|plurality]] of the vote.<ref name="nbcnews">{{cite news |title=Chile presidential race headed to runoff between communist and hard-right candidates |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/world/chile/chile-votes-presidential-poll-pitting-communist-far-right-rcna244186 |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=[[NBC News]] |date=16 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=16 November 2025 |title=Trump-Style Candidate Heads to runoff in Chile's Election |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/16/world/americas/chile-presidential-election-on-sunday.html |access-date=17 November 2025 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> Kast, who has been described as [[conservative]] or [[Far-right politics|hard-right]], placed second.<ref name="nbcnews" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 November 2025 |title=Communist and far-right candidates head to Chile presidential runoff |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0jd0v8dvpwo |access-date=20 November 2025 |website=BBC |language=en-GB |quote=Kast has pledged to build ditches along Chile's northern border with Peru and Bolivia, as well as mass deportations of undocumented migrants and people who entered the country illegally. He has also promised new maximum-security prisons, like those built in El Salvador.}}</ref> Combined, right-leaning candidates received approximately 70 percent of the first-round presidential vote.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nugent |first=Ciara |date=26 November 2025 |title=Far right poised to win Chile's presidency |url=https://www.ft.com/content/4d9abd84-b792-4b60-a59a-390192a8c8c5 |access-date=16 November 2025 |website=Financial Times}}</ref> [[Johannes Kaiser (Chilean politician)|Johannes Kaiser]] and [[Evelyn Matthei]], both candidates on the right,<ref name="LAR-20251117" /> endorsed Kast for the runoff, while [[Franco Parisi]] declined to make an endorsement, calling on his voters to decide themselves who to vote for.<ref name="bbc1" /> In the runoff, Kast defeated Jara in a [[landslide victory]] with 58% of the vote, the second-highest percentage of the vote since Chile's transition to democracy. Kast received 7.2 million votes, which was the highest ever vote total in Chile's history. He won in all sixteen [[Regions of Chile|regions of the country]].<ref name="tercera1" />

In the parliamentary election, [[Unidad por Chile]], a coalition of the ruling leftist and center-left parties, emerged as the largest bloc of the Chamber of Deputies, while in the Senate the left-wing and right-wing blocs reached a tie with 25 senators each.<ref name="infodep">{{cite news |last1=Palazzo |first1=Mauricio |title=Así quedó el Congreso chileno tras las elecciones parlamentarias |url=https://www.infobae.com/america/america-latina/2025/11/17/asi-quedo-el-congreso-chileno-tras-las-elecciones-parlamentarias/ |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=Infobae |date=17 November 2025}}</ref><ref name="uchile" />

This was the first general election since the reintroduction of [[compulsory voting]] in 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Palacios |first=Cristóbal |date=19 December 2022 |title=Se repone voto obligatorio: Congreso aprueba reforma que dejará atrás el sufragio voluntario a 10 años de su implementación |url=https://www.latercera.com/politica/noticia/se-repone-voto-obligatorio-congreso-aprueba-reforma-que-dejara-atras-el-sufragio-voluntario-a-10-anos-de-su-implementacion/BG47J7ZH6ZGQTODHYCYVAJ5I6E/ |access-date=16 November 2025 |website=La Tercera |language=es}}</ref>

== Background ==
{{main|2021 Chilean general election}}
The [[2021 Chilean general election|2021 presidential election]] saw left-wing candidate [[Gabriel Boric]], a former student protest leader during the [[2011–2013 Chilean student protests|2011–2013 student protests]], defeat conservative rival [[José Antonio Kast]] in a runoff. Boric's victory was attributed to widespread youth mobilization, dissatisfaction with the center-right administration of [[Sebastián Piñera]], and economic strains following the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Chile|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Bonnefoy |first1=Pascale |last2=Londoño |first2=Ernesto |date=19 December 2021 |title=Gabriel Boric, a Former Student Activist, Is Elected Chile's Youngest President |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/19/world/americas/chile-president-election.html |access-date=17 July 2023 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> His platform emphasized social equity, feminist reforms, and a "dignified life" for marginalized groups, pledging to dismantle Chile's "patriarchal inheritance".<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 December 2021 |title=Leftist millennial vows to remake Chile after historic win |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2021/12/20/boric-leftist-millennial-vows-to-remake-chile-after-historic-win.html |access-date=17 July 2023 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Cambero |first1=Fabian |last2=Esposito |first2=Anthony |last3=Miranda |first3=Natalia A. Ramos |date=20 December 2021 |title=Chile's Boric pledges an orderly economy, swift naming of Cabinet |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/chiles-left-cheers-boric-win-ripples-through-latin-america-2021-12-20/ |access-date=17 July 2023 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref>

By mid-2023, Boric's approval ratings had plummeted to 28%, with 66% disapproving of his administration, according to polls.<ref>{{Cite news |date=27 June 2023 |title=Chile's President Boric Takes Another Blow to Popularity as Graft Accusations Hit Key Allies |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-06-27/chile-s-boric-sees-approval-fall-after-graft-accusations-against-coalition-party |access-date=17 July 2023 |work=Bloomberg.com |language=en}}</ref> Analysts cited economic stagnation, legislative gridlock, and public safety concerns as primary factors. The right-wing [[Republican Party (Chile)|Republican Party]], led by Kast, capitalized on this discontent, securing a dominant victory in the [[2023 Chilean Constitutional Council election|May 2023 Constitutional Council elections]], resulting in the failed [[2023 Chilean constitutional referendum]]. Kast declared the results a mandate for "a major change in government", positioning himself as a frontrunner for 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Chile's far right re-emerges after presidential defeat |url=https://www.batimes.com.ar/news/latin-america/chiles-far-right-re-emerges-after-presidential-defeat.phtml |access-date=17 July 2023 |website=Buenos Aires Times |date=11 May 2023 }}</ref> Meanwhile, [[Evelyn Matthei]] of the [[Independent Democratic Union]] (UDI) also gained traction in opinion polls.<ref>{{Cite web |last=de la Fuente |first=Antonieta |date=9 May 2023 |title=Explaining the rise of the far-right Republican Party in Chile |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2023-05-09/explaining-the-rise-of-the-far-right-republican-party-in-chile.html |access-date=17 July 2023 |website=EL PAÍS English |language=en-us}}</ref>

== Electoral system ==
This was the first presidential and congressional election since a constitutional amendment was passed and promulgated on 27 December 2022, restoring [[compulsory voting]] for all elections and plebiscites for the first time since 2012, except in primary elections.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ley 21524 Firma electrónica MODIFICA LA CARTA FUNDAMENTAL PARA RESTABLECER EL VOTO OBLIGATORIO EN LAS ELECCIONES POPULARES |url=https://www.bcn.cl/leychile/navegar?idNorma=1187324&idVersion=2023-01-04 |access-date=4 August 2025 |website=[[Library of the National Congress of Chile]]}}</ref> Eligible citizens who did not vote faced a fine of approximately 33,000 [[Chilean peso|pesos]] (around US$35).<ref>{{Cite web |title=What To Know About the Results of Chile's 2024 Municipal and Regional Elections |url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/what-know-about-results-chiles-2024-municipal-and-regional-elections |access-date=4 August 2025 |website=Americas Society/[[Council of the Americas]] |date=29 October 2024 }}</ref>

=== President ===
The President is elected using the [[two-round system]]; if no candidate receives over 50% of the vote to win outright in the first round, a second round will be held.<ref name="spo">{{cite web |title=Chili : Système électoral |url=https://www.sciencespo.fr/opalc/node/920.html |website=[[Sciences Po]] |access-date=15 December 2025}}</ref>

=== National Congress ===
* Chamber of Deputies: The 155 members are elected from 28 multi-member constituencies (3–8 seats each) using [[open-list proportional representation]] with the [[d'Hondt method]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Dote |first1=Sebastián |title=Elecciones parlamentarias de Chile 2025: cómo se eligen a los diputados, a los senadores y todo lo que hay que saber |url=https://elpais.com/chile/2025-11-14/elecciones-parlamentarias-de-chile-2025-como-se-eligen-a-los-diputados-a-los-senadores-y-todo-lo-que-hay-que-saber.html |access-date=16 December 2025 |work=El País |date=15 November 2025}}</ref>
* Senate: The 50 senators serve staggered eight-year terms. Half the chamber (25 seats) is renewed each general election, elected from 16 regional constituencies (2–5 seats each) under similar proportional rules.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Political structure |url=http://country.eiu.com/article.aspx?articleid=322002015&Country=Chile&topic=Summary&subtopic=Poli_5 |access-date=17 July 2023 |website=country.eiu.com}}</ref>

